# project-bootstrap (Cursor)

Turns "an agent just landed in an unfamiliar repo" into "an agent that
knows the stack, has verified the project runs, and has documentation to
prove it" — a one-time foundation step, not an every-session ritual.
Unlike a plain doc generator, this actually installs dependencies and tries
running the project, so what gets written reflects commands just verified
to work.

## When NOT to use

`CLAUDE.md`/`AGENTS.md` already exists and is up to date — just read it.
Installing *skills* from a catalog into another project is a different
task entirely. A large monorepo with many subprojects on different stacks
— run this per relevant subfolder instead of one combined summary.

## Steps

1. Detect the stack: package manager via lockfile, other stack markers
   (Python/Go/Rust/PHP/Ruby/Java/Elixir), existing docs, CI config,
   containers, tests, linting. No marker matching is itself a signal —
   check subfolders manually, it might be a monorepo.
2. Confirm the install plan with the user before executing — installing
   dependencies has real side effects (arbitrary install scripts). Show
   the command, get confirmation.
3. Install dependencies with the detected command. If it fails, report the
   error as-is — don't proceed pretending it worked.
4. Verify the project actually runs: build command (cheapest signal), test
   suite if one exists (record the honest result, doesn't need to be
   all-green), dev server/start command briefly to confirm no crash on
   startup, then stop it.
5. Write or update `CLAUDE.md`/`AGENTS.md`: if it exists, offer to add a
   new section rather than overwrite; if not, offer to create one with a
   project summary, detected stack, and install/build/test/dev commands
   that were just verified — not guessed — noting anything that failed.
   Always confirm before writing a new file into the user's repo.
6. Optionally suggest relevant skills from a catalog matching the detected
   stack — offer, don't install automatically.
